Warps let the GPU execute one instruction across 32 threads efficiently, rather than managing each thread completely separately. If those threads take different branches, the GPU must execute the different paths with only the relevant threads active, which wastes some execution capacity. Accessing consecutive memory locations also helps because the GPU can combine the threads’ memory requests into fewer transactions, improving memory throughput.

